# Implement the user-controlled Proton Mail web adapter

## Goal

Provide the primary Free-plan local provider path through Proton Mail's ordinary
web application without requiring Bridge or a third-party direct API identity.

## Required work

- Use a dedicated project browser profile instead of attaching to an existing
  personal browser profile.
- Keep sign-in visible and fully user-controlled; password, second factor,
  CAPTCHA, recovery, and security-key interactions remain outside automation.
- Begin mailbox automation only after the dedicated profile is already signed
  in and the expected Proton Mail application state is visible.
- Never export cookies, local storage, session state, passwords, or challenge
  answers into MCP, config files, fixtures, or logs.
- Implement provider-neutral read, draft, send, reversible mailbox actions, and
  change observation through explicit UI state assertions.
- Prefer semantic roles, labels, and observable application state over brittle
  styling selectors.
- Detect unexpected pages, selector drift, login expiry, and provider changes;
  fail closed instead of clicking a guessed control.
- Require exclusive ownership of the dedicated profile while managed browser
  automation is active. If the profile is locked by the visible login browser,
  fail with a typed retryable state instead of attaching to another profile or
  silently creating a fresh session.
- Keep draft creation and send as distinct actions under the existing
  capability/authority model.

## Acceptance criteria

- A Proton Free account can establish the dedicated profile through visible
  manual login with no Bridge dependency.
- Existing browser profiles are never read or modified.
- Tests prove password and authentication challenge fields are never targeted.
- Read-only workflows cannot trigger mutation or send controls.
- Draft and send remain separately authorized and observable.
- Selector or page-shape drift fails closed before a side effect.
- An opt-in live account can execute every capability advertised by this
  adapter.

## Evidence

- Dedicated-profile lifecycle tests
- Synthetic page-state and selector-drift fixtures
- Read-vs-mutation control boundary tests
- Draft/send side-effect tests
- Redacted opt-in Free-account transcript

## Require complete accessibility shell identities

- The Chrome DevTools `Accessibility.AXNode` schema requires a string `nodeId`,
  `ignored` boolean and typed `role`. Red-first fake-browser tests proved the
  Mail shell previously accepted a visible search landmark with no node ID
  or role at all. Shell readiness now requires a bounded, nonempty, printable
  node ID, matching typed role, and unique node IDs within each role query,
  including ignored nodes. Incomplete or contradictory CDP evidence fails
  closed, while valid synthetic Chromium replies, role-count limits and
  late-origin checks remain supported without projecting accessible names.

## Reject cross-role accessibility node aliases

- Red-first fake-browser evidence showed the same CDP accessibility node ID
  could satisfy both navigation and search queries with incompatible roles.
  The bounded Mail-shell classifier now requires IDs unique across all four
  role queries, including ignored dialog nodes, rather than only within each
  response. Distinct-role fixtures still prove ready and blocked states, and
  no accessible names or account data are retained.

## Reject contradictory accessibility hidden reasons

- Red-first synthetic AX role queries showed a node marked `ignored:false`
  could still report a nonempty `ignoredReasons` array and establish Mail
  shell readiness. The bounded classifier now rejects that contradiction,
  validates optional reason metadata even on ignored nodes, and retains
  ordinary visible nodes with empty reasons. No accessible names are read.

## Require audited AXValue role properties

- Red-first synthetic browser roles showed a valid-looking navigation
  landmark with an unreviewed nested or null-valued AXValue role property
  could still prove the Mail shell. Role proof now permits only the four
  documented AXValue keys, with bounded optional related-node and source
  arrays; malformed or unknown fields fail closed. Ordinary bare roles and
  optional empty arrays remain compatible without projecting accessible text.

## Validate ignored accessibility reason semantics

- Red-first Chromium fixtures showed an ignored AX node could report an
  ordinary property name or unknown reason and AXValue type yet still pass
  shell inspection. The role classifier now requires documented hidden reason
  names, known AXValue types and exact AXProperty field shapes; reason metadata
  extensions fail closed. Valid normal hidden reasons remain compatible and
  accessible names are still not projected into Mail-shell evidence.

## Validate AXValue collection elements before shell readiness

- Red-first fake-browser roles and hidden reasons proved non-object
  `relatedNodes` entries, missing backend node IDs, and unknown `sources`
  types could still establish a Mail shell. Shared AXValue checks now bound
  both collections, validate related backend identity, and require documented
  source types and known outer properties. Canonical optional source and
  related-node objects remain accepted without retaining their content.

## Disqualify explicitly insecure network response provenance

- The shared passive Network response gate accepted an otherwise valid
  provider GET even if CDP explicitly marked its `securityState` as insecure,
  unknown, or malformed. Red-first synthetic response fixtures now require a
  reported resource security state to be exactly `secure`. Omission remains
  compatible with older CDP fixtures; all eight passive read collectors share
  this check, with no certificate, header, or mailbox content retained.
  Unreviewed security-state spelling variants and extensions also fail closed,
  rather than bypassing a valid `securityState` with a contradictory alias.

## Reject additional unreviewed cache and response-source markers

- Red-first synthetic direct-provider HTTP metadata still passed when unknown
  cache hints used `isFrom...`, `servedFrom...`, `wasServedFrom...`, `cache...`,
  `prefetch...`, or `source...` keys outside the existing source flag prefix
  checks, including contradictory true values. The shared passive response
  gate now rejects these unreviewed source-key families case-insensitively,
  including false-valued aliases. The documented exact CDP flags remain
  compatible, so all passive collectors share the same stricter provenance.

## Require canonical URL characters in browser origin metadata

- Red-first synthetic `TargetInfo.url` and DOM `documentURL` evidence could
  contain raw whitespace, controls, or Unicode and still pass the provider
  origin prefix check. Those characters must be serialized in percent-encoded
  form in browser-owned URLs; unescaped values are incompatible metadata and
  can be reinterpreted by URL parsers. Shared URL-origin validation now rejects
  non-printable or non-ASCII bytes while keeping valid percent-encoded routes.

## Validate nested AXNode computed property collections

- Red-first synthetic role queries accepted arbitrary objects and malformed
  or oversized arrays in the documented `AXNode.properties` field while still
  proving a ready shell. The classifier now requires bounded, unique, known
  `AXPropertyName` entries with structurally valid `AXValue` metadata. It
  rejects unknown or duplicate names, incomplete entries, nested extensions,
  and composite scalar values without projecting private accessible text.

## Reject unknown AXNode fields before shell classification

- Red-first synthetic role-query evidence showed that a node with unknown
  extension fields, including nested private data, still established a ready
  Mail shell. The role classifier now admits only documented CDP AXNode keys,
  while accepting ordinary optional role/name/description/value/properties
  fields without returning or retaining their content. Unknown extensions
  cannot silently expand the browser trust boundary.

## Refuse unreviewed compound AX scalar values

- Red-first synthetic role and ignored-reason evidence showed AXValue types
  outside the initial scalar guards could include an arbitrary object or array
  and still pass shell inspection. `AXValue.value` is a CDP `any`, but complex
  payloads are unnecessary for this structural proof. The shared value guard
  now rejects unaudited composite payloads in nested source values and hidden
  reasons, while preserving ordinary scalars and absent optional values.

## Reject accessibility cycles expressed through child IDs

- Red-first synthetic Mail-shell replies accepted two role nodes that named
  each other in `childIds` without any `parentId`. A mixed three-node cycle
  using both explicit child ownership and a parent claim was also possible.
  Shell proof now checks one combined child-to-parent graph across all four
  role queries. Acyclic child-only chains and unobserved descendants remain
  compatible, without accessing accessible names or browser DOM text.

## Reject conflicting AX child ownership across role replies

- Red-first synthetic role queries accepted an explicitly claimed child with a
  different reported parent, or two distinct role nodes both claiming one
  child ID. Cross-query shell evidence now checks explicit child ownership
  against observed parent IDs and rejects multiple owners. Missing parent or
  child entries still remain normal for a role-filtered query; a consistent
  navigation/search pair passes without retaining accessibility text.

## Reject cycles across accessibility role query results

- Red-first synthetic Mail-shell queries allowed navigation/search role nodes
  to name each other as their parent, even though each node separately passed
  local relationship checks. The role-query aggregator now rejects cycles in
  the bounded set of explicit AX parent links across all four responses.
  Ordinary acyclic links, including references to nodes outside filtered
  results, remain accepted without exposing accessible names or DOM text.

## Validate typed accessibility source metadata

- Red-first fake-browser roles accepted optional AXValueSource fields with
  numeric attributes, string booleans, unknown native-source enums, and
  untyped nested AXValue payloads while claiming a ready Mail shell. These
  fields now require documented CDP shapes; recursive source values have a
  small bounded nesting budget. Documented optional source fields, including
  nested related-node identities, remain accepted. The same checks protect
  hidden-reason source metadata without projecting accessible names.

## Reject contradictory accessibility reason value types

- Red-first synthetic AX replies showed ignored nodes could report boolean
  reasons as strings or null, and optional source/related-node collections as
  non-array values, without causing a protocol failure. The classifier now
  checks common scalar AXValue payload types and bounds optional arrays as
  well as the previously audited names and type enums. Valid hidden boolean
  reasons and legitimate structural roles remain accepted without displaying
  or retaining their private attributes.

## Require coherent accessibility node relationships

- Red-first fake-browser roles could prove Mail shell readiness even when
  an `AXNode` reported itself as its parent or listed itself as a child.
  Optional parent and child IDs now use the same bounded printable identity
  guard as the node itself; self-links, duplicate children and malformed
  metadata fail closed, while ordinary external relationships remain valid.
  The driver does not traverse private accessibility subtrees.

## Refuse reversed accessibility parent-child relationships

- A red-first fake-browser navigation role claimed one external node ID as
  both its parent and a child. Although neither referred to the node itself,
  this is an impossible tree relationship and previously established a ready
  Mail shell. Parent IDs now cannot also appear among their node's children;
  a valid distinct parent and children remain compatible.

## Bound optional accessibility backend-node IDs

- Red-first fake-browser role responses showed a valid-looking navigation
  landmark with a zero, negative, string, fractional, or oversized optional
  CDP `backendDOMNodeId` still established a ready Mail shell. The classifier
  now requires any reported backend DOM identity to be a positive signed
  32-bit protocol integer. Omitted identities and in-range IDs remain valid;
  no accessible names, DOM text, or live account data are inspected.

## Keep AX frame identities aligned with CDP endpoint syntax

- Red-first fake-browser evidence showed `AXNode.frameId` could report a
  separator-bearing string (`/`, `?`, or `:`) and still prove shell readiness.
  Unlike an AX node identifier, a browser frame ID is a bounded endpoint
  identity and now requires ASCII letters, digits, hyphens, or underscores.
  Normal frame tokens and omitted optional frame metadata remain valid.

## Refuse malformed optional accessibility frame identities

- Red-first fake-browser query results proved a search/navigation landmark
  could report an empty, numeric, null, control-bearing, or oversized optional
  CDP `frameId` and still establish Mail-shell readiness. Optional frame IDs
  now have bounded printable ASCII identity syntax. Missing frame IDs remain
  compatible, and a normal browser frame token still establishes a shell
  without reading accessible names or message content.

## Require full document-root evidence before AX inspection

- Red-first synthetic Chromium evidence showed a bare `DOM.getDocument`
  numeric node ID previously authorized accessibility inspection. The CDP
  document node also needs a backend identity, document type, name, local
  name, and value. Node IDs must fit the positive signed protocol integer
  range in both browser responses and subsequent accessibility requests.
  Missing or contradictory evidence fails closed; optional metadata remains
  compatible without reading private mailbox information.

## Bound optional CDP document-root child counts

- Red-first protocol and fake-browser responses showed a `DOM.getDocument`
  root with a negative, fractional, string, null, or oversized `childNodeCount`
  still established Mail-shell readiness. The optional child count now must be
  a nonnegative signed 32-bit CDP integer; missing and valid counts remain
  compatible. This does not read DOM descendants or private mailbox text.

## Validate optional CDP document metadata shapes

- Red-first protocol and fake-browser tests showed arbitrary values for
  optional `DOM.Node.compatibilityMode` and `baseURL` could establish
  Mail-shell readiness. These document fields now require respectively an
  audited CDP enum and a bounded string. The base URL may legitimately point
  off origin, so it is never used as origin proof; missing fields remain
  compatible.

## Reject frame-owner identity in document roots

- A red-first protocol and fake-browser regression showed that a document
  root with a syntactically valid `frameId` still proved a ready Mail shell.
  CDP reserves `DOM.Node.frameId` for frame-owner elements, not document
  nodes. Any such field now fails closed regardless of shape, while valid
  document metadata without a frame-owner identity stays supported.

## Refuse non-document node metadata in CDP roots

- Red-first CDP and synthetic-browser fixtures showed that a claimed
  `#document` node with attribute (`name`, `value`), document-type (`publicId`,
  `systemId`, `internalSubset`), or pseudo-element (`pseudoIdentifier`)
  metadata still authorized Mail shell queries. Those are not document-node
  properties and now fail closed; normal optional document metadata remains
  compatible without accessing DOM descendants.

## Reject element-only metadata on document roots

- Red-first CDP schema and fake-browser fixtures showed that a top-level
  document could carry frame-owner, template, HTML import, distribution,
  element attribute, or slot metadata yet still authorize Mail-shell AX
  inspection. `DOM.Node` reserves those fields for non-document elements.
  The parser now refuses these contradictory fields; document metadata
  such as URL and compatibility mode stays independently compatible.

## Refuse unsolicited CDP document-root subtrees

- Red-first protocol and synthetic-browser cases proved that a depth-zero
  `DOM.getDocument` reply could carry nested `children`, `pseudoElements`, or
  `shadowRoots` and still authorize Mail-shell accessibility inspection.
  Such subtrees were not requested and may contain private DOM data. The
  parser now rejects them even when the top-level document identity is valid;
  canonical depth-zero roots continue to work.

## Refuse unreviewed document-root properties

- Red-first private CDP and fake-browser tests showed depth-zero document
  results could include an arbitrary unknown property carrying nested private
  data while still proving a ready Mail shell. The parser now admits only the
  required document identity and the already audited optional child count,
  URL, base URL and compatibility mode. Other properties, including
  null-valued extensions and element-only SVG flags, fail closed before AX
  queries. Ordinary optional document metadata remains supported.

## Reject contradictory document-root kinds

- Red-first protocol and fake-browser tests showed a top-level CDP document
  could carry a parent, pseudo-element classification, or shadow-root type
  yet still authorize the subsequent Mail-shell accessibility queries.
  `DOM.getDocument` root validation now rejects these contradictory node
  identities while retaining ordinary optional document metadata. This
  requires no private DOM text or live provider interaction.

## Corroborate optional document URLs before shell inspection

- Red-first protocol and fake-browser fixtures showed `DOM.getDocument`
  accepted a foreign-origin `documentURL` despite verified Mail location.
  Optional document URLs now must be bounded and belong to the exact Mail
  origin before a root can authorize accessibility queries. Missing URLs
  remain compatible, and no route text is retained or logged.

## Exact shape for browser-origin evidence

- A red-first fake-Chromium CDP reply proved that an extended location
  projection with an unexpected property still classified as trusted Mail.
  The driver now requires exactly the three properties produced by its static
  location script before checking protocol, hostname and port. This fails
  closed on runtime reply drift without accepting new origins or selectors.

## Unambiguous by-value browser evidence

- Red-first fake-Chromium replies proved that `Runtime.evaluate` accepted
  an apparently valid location value alongside a conflicting CDP `objectId`
  or `unserializableValue`. The read-only driver now rejects these mutually
  exclusive RemoteObject representations before establishing trusted page
  origin or using other UI evidence. Synthetic regressions cover both variants
  without inspecting a live browser profile.

## Reject alternate deep serialization of by-value page evidence

- Red-first fake-browser regression proved `Runtime.evaluate` accepted a
  valid Mail `value` alongside a contradictory CDP `deepSerializedValue`.
  The return-by-value path now rejects that alternate representation just
  as it rejects remote handles and unserializable primitives. Chromium is
  only asked for one bounded by-value result; no browser account is used.

## Restrict by-value Runtime object descriptors

- Red-first synthetic CDP origins accepted unreviewed `RemoteObject` keys and
  malformed `className`/`description` metadata while proving Mail origin.
  Return-by-value results now admit only audited descriptors with bounded
  optional string metadata; a valid `Object` class/description still works.
  Unexpected metadata cannot silently introduce a second browser-owned data
  channel alongside the static origin projection.

## Reject unsolicited runtime object previews

- Red-first synthetic browser replies carried a plausible trusted-origin
  `value` together with an unrequested `preview` or `customPreview`.
  The audited `Runtime.evaluate` requests only return-by-value JSON and
  does not enable object preview or custom formatting. Both extra CDP
  representations now fail closed before origin or shell authority, without
  retaining or logging any additional object properties.

## Unambiguous mailbox-catalog cursor accounts

- The browser-owned catalog continuation codec now applies the same bounded
  provider-neutral event-cursor account policy before snapshot reads or token
  encoding/decoding. Red-first synthetic tests reject invisible, whitespace,
  and control-bearing account IDs; valid printable Unicode and the exact
  512-byte UTF-8 limit remain compatible. No live mailbox is accessed.

## Reject noncharacters before browser row serialization

- Red-first Node execution of both Mail list-ID and content-bearing row
  expressions reproduced non-interchange Unicode scalars being returned as
  page IDs, subject titles, and sender addresses before the strict Rust parse.
  Both scripts now reject U+FDD0..U+FDEF and all Unicode plane-ending
  noncharacters while still in the trusted-origin browser task. Row IDs are
  checked before accessing any subject or sender; ordinary visible Unicode
  and emoji continue to project; rejected values never cross CDP.

## Stable glyphs in displayed Mail headers and rows

- Red-first display-guard, synthetic message-detail, and visible-row checks
  showed Unicode noncharacters passing as subjects, sender metadata, and
  address-row labels. The shared const text guard now refuses these across
  all Unicode planes, while retaining language joiners, variation selectors,
  and emoji graphemes. The same predicate guards catalog display names.

## Invisible search-mode selector refusal

- A real Node evaluation of the browser-owned message-mode expression
  reproduced a noncharacter-only search hash being treated as proof of a
  message list. The same selector can be present on a forced message route.
  The script now rejects non-interchange Unicode noncharacters across all
  planes before treating decoded search text as reliable mode evidence;
  ordinary visible Unicode searches remain valid. No page content leaves the
  browser or live provider login is attempted.

## Noncharacter refusal for route-bound Mail IDs

- Red-first synthetic message-list and detail responses accepted Unicode
  noncharacters (`U+FDD0`, `U+FFFE`, and `U+10FFFF`) as opaque IDs that could
  later be reused as exact URL path components. The shared ID guard now
  rejects the entire `U+FDD0..U+FDEF` range and the final two code points of
  every Unicode plane; ordinary printable opaque IDs remain compatible.
  This does not reinterpret or normalize provider identities.

## Trusting the complete browser origin

- The public `PageOrigin` policy now requires the normalized `location.port`
  as well as scheme and hostname. Previously the managed CDP driver did check
  the port privately, but another policy caller could classify a non-default
  HTTPS port as trusted by passing only scheme and hostname. The single shared
  policy rejects alternate ports and malformed spellings while retaining the
  default HTTPS origin and the exact `443` spelling. Offline synthetic tests
  cover both Mail and Account hostnames; no browser login is automated.

## Bind passive browser GETs to an explicit Mail document origin

- A synthetic Network.requestWillBeSent message-list event carrying a valid
  Mail API GET URL but a foreign `documentURL` previously became a tracked
  first-page request. The common read provenance predicate now requires any
  reported requesting document URL to be a bounded browser-serialized Mail
  HTTPS origin, not a foreign frame, Account origin, alternate port, or
  malformed/Unicode URL. Red-first shared and message-list tests prove that
  the foreign-document request fails while a first-party Mail document works.

- All eight read observers already use the common predicate. Legacy CDP
  events omitting the optional document hint remain supported; this check
  therefore cannot prove main-frame ownership for omitted hints or same-origin
  subframes. No request URLs or message content are persisted by the guard.

## Refuse unknown or non-read CDP request classifications

- A red-first message-list GET with ResourceType `FedCM` or lowercase
  `prefetch` previously passed as a normal active read. Shared CDP request
  provenance now permits only reviewed non-speculative ResourceType enum
  values and denies preload, preflight, federated-auth, and unknown types.

- Request initiator types also use exact reviewed enum values, not a denylist
  that overlooks `Preload` case aliases or future initiator classes. Synthetic
  tests preserve `Fetch`, `XHR`, ordinary `script`/`parser`, and missing legacy
  metadata. All eight read observers share this rule without retaining the
  URL, headers, or request body.

## Type the response event before consuming passive read evidence

- A synthetic message-list lifecycle with a normal `Fetch` GET but a
  `Network.responseReceived` ResourceType of `Prefetch` previously reached
  successful response state. Every passive observer now checks the response
  event's optional ResourceType against the same closed non-speculative enum
  before admitting provider HTTP provenance.

- Shared unit and message-list regressions reject prefetch, preflight, FedCM,
  invalid spellings, and future types while preserving ordinary XHR/Fetch and
  omitted legacy hints. This does not claim that request and response types
  are byte-for-byte identical, or prove an absent hint from browser metadata.

## Current implementation

- The Mail shell's `DOM.getDocument` root now refuses contradictory CDP
  node metadata (`nodeType` other than document, wrong node/local names, or
  nonempty node value) before asking the accessibility tree for readiness.
  Red-first fake-browser tests cover malformed roots and valid ordinary
  document roots; omitted optional metadata remains compatible.

- The trusted-sticky-parent synthetic login test now observes the complete
  detached browser argv before deleting its test profile. The CLI can return
  before the fake browser finishes writing; premature cleanup occasionally
  raised `DirectoryNotEmpty` and discarded an otherwise valid safety proof.
  The test also confirms the actual private profile directory argument.

- The CDP `Browser.getVersion` handshake now admits only the five audited
  optional/required string fields with individual byte ceilings, rejecting
  unexpected or malformed metadata before the browser's version marker can
  establish protocol readiness. Red-first fake-browser cases preserve standard
  Chromium version fields and reject unfamiliar authority extensions without
  retaining version strings or contacting a live provider.

- The synthetic visible-login stdio-isolation regression now inspects the
  launched browser's own `/proc/<pid>/fd/0` and requires `/dev/null`. A CLI
  that exits before its test parent writes to the pipe may legitimately cause
  `BrokenPipe`; this no longer aborts the test before the stronger browser-fd
  proof and output-channel isolation checks. Production launch flags stay the
  same; no live browser or account is accessed.

- Shared passive Network response provenance now rejects unknown or differently
  cased `from*`, `serviceWorker*`, and `cacheStorage*` metadata fields, rather
  than treating future cached/service-worker markers as direct provider HTTP.
  Red-first synthetic tests cover affirmative, false, and null source hints
  while preserving ordinary non-provenance response metadata. Every read-only
  capture using the shared gate inherits this fail-closed behavior.

- The selected Mail or Account `TargetInfo` must contain only audited CDP
  metadata fields. An unexpected future authority/lifecycle property must not
  silently classify a provider page as the dedicated user-controlled tab.
  Red-first synthetic browser replies cover both provider origins, preserve
  standard optional title/attachment fields, reject malformed/oversized
  title metadata without retaining its contents, and ignore unrelated worker
  extensions without selecting them; no real account was accessed.

- Root-page selection now refuses provider targets with CDP opener/parent/
  opener-frame relationships or affirmative/malformed opener access. A popup
  opened by a script is not equivalent to the dedicated visible Mail tab,
  even if it points to the same HTTPS provider origin. Red-first fake-browser
  regressions cover Mail and Account target metadata and retain an explicit
  `canAccessOpener: false` ordinary target; no live account was accessed.

- Selected Mail and Account targets now reject a nonempty or malformed CDP
  `TargetInfo.subtype`: a prerendered `page` with the same provider URL is not
  evidence of a visible user-controlled tab. Red-first fake-browser cases
  cover both origins, prefetched/special target markers, bad types, and a
  normal page with an empty optional subtype. No real browser was accessed.

- The bounded `Target.getTargets` catalog now requires a valid unique target ID
  on every entry, including unrelated workers and non-provider pages. Missing
  IDs could previously pass alongside a valid Mail tab, weakening the complete
  catalog identity proof. A red-first fake Chromium regression rejects absent,
  null, numeric, and empty IDs on both sides of the chosen page while retaining
  valid unrelated target types; no real account was accessed.

- Outbound CDP commands now enforce the inbound one-MiB JSON ceiling
  plus their NUL delimiter while serializing through guarded storage.
  Oversized synthetic runtime commands fail with redacted protocol errors;
  a writer-limit fixture confirms rejected bytes do not modify the guarded
  private prefix. Ordinary requests and response framing remain unchanged.

- CDP inbound frame assembly now uses the same scrub-on-growth buffer
  as serialized commands. Partial browser frames spanning the read cache can
  no longer release older private frame prefixes during Vec expansion; the
  bounded NUL parser and absolute timeout behavior stay intact. A synthetic
  multi-cache fixture checks both passive and ordinary reader paths.

- CDP outbound command serialization now routes through a scrub-on-growth
  writer. When request JSON exceeds an initial vector allocation, each old
  private prefix is erased before its storage is freed. The NUL frame marker
  uses the same guarded append path. Synthetic short and long request frames
  preserve exact JSON and framing with no browser/account access.

- The private CDP frame reader now uses an 8 KiB scrub-on-drop read-ahead
  cache instead of the standard buffered stream. Consumed frame bytes are
  erased immediately, while coalesced unread replies remain available until
  parsed or dropped. Raw frame output and outbound bytes retain their existing
  zeroizing custody. Synthetic tests prove cache scrubbing after complete and
  partial/deadline-failed replies and recovery after idle socket timeouts;
  browser-side and kernel copies are outside this Rust allocation boundary.

- The managed browser's flattened CDP session token now leaves `attach()` in
  `Zeroizing<String>`, and both attach/detach command parameter objects stay
  in the scrub-on-drop strict JSON guard. This prevents ordinary intermediate
  session and selected-target copies in addition to guarded frame serialization;
  existing origin revalidation and command permission gates are unchanged.

- The managed browser's selected target identity now scrubs its CDP ID on
  drop, including origin/mode rejection paths. Its public origin classifier
  and fail-closed page targeting behavior are unchanged.

- Bounded visible Mail row snapshots now scrub copied row IDs, subjects, and
  displayed correspondent strings when dropped; list-state evidence also
  erases opaque IDs during unstable-page rejection. The duplicate-ID guard
  borrows input JSON strings instead of leaving ordinary-heap HashSet clones.
  Red-first Drop checks and existing page/pagination fixtures preserve behavior.
  This guards Rust-side copies, not Chromium DOM or OS allocations.

- The Linux Chromium SingletonLock unreaped-PID regression now waits for
  actual zombie evidence within a bounded two-second deadline rather than
  assuming /bin/true is scheduled in 100ms under workspace test load. The
  test also reaps its synthetic child on a failed precondition; production
  singleton and lease policy remain unchanged.

- The synthetic visible-login lease-during-ETXTBSY regression now
  keeps the executable write lock until the runner exits. Releasing it
  immediately after acquiring the lease could let an already-checked
  browser spawn slip through before the next lease check and cause a
  nondeterministic test failure. Production lease policy is unchanged.


- CDP NUL-delimited frame readers now apply an absolute timeout per frame,
  based on the active socket wait budget. Partial data arriving just before
  each syscall timeout cannot keep a command or passive event capture alive
  indefinitely; the socket timeout is restored before subsequent cleanup.
  Synthetic slow-drip pipe tests cover both ordinary and passive readers.
- Visible login retries only transient executable-text-busy (`ETXTBSY`)
  failures with a finite 320 ms budget, matching the managed-browser pattern.
  A red-first test holds a fake executable writable during login and verifies
  launch succeeds after the descriptor is released; other errors still fail.
  Every retry also rechecks the managed automation lease, so a lease acquired
  during the delay blocks delayed visible launch. Synthetic parallel lease
  acquisition proves the earlier single-check gap no longer authorizes launch.
- Visible-login Chromium runs with stdin, stdout, and stderr detached from
  the invoking CLI. A synthetic fake-browser process confirms a child cannot
  read piped CLI input or inject its diagnostics into captured CLI channels.
- `protonmail-ai auth login` launches Proton Mail in a fixed, project-owned
  Chromium profile using `--user-data-dir`; it does not attach to a personal
  browser profile or enable remote debugging.
- Absolute XDG data-home resolution, HOME fallback, `0700` Linux permissions,
  executable discovery, and symlink/non-directory rejection are implemented.
- Eleven process tests exercise profile custody and launch behavior with a
  fake browser; active managed automation blocks visible login before browser
  launch, and no live browser/account is used by the deterministic suite.
- `AutomationProfileLease` classifies Chromium `SingletonLock` ownership,
  refuses active/foreign/malformed owners without deleting Chromium state, and
  serializes managed drivers with a `0600` PID/start-time lease, safely reclaims
