- Local automation lease owners now share canonical decimal validation
  with Chromium singleton targets; signed and leading-zero PID/start-tick
  variants fail as corrupt entries instead of being silently reclaimed.
- Stable visible row projection now re-reads the same bounded row fields
  in one target session and refuses subject, correspondent, or unread drift
  even when content-free list evidence retains identical row IDs. A fake
  browser regression reproduces subject changes without any ID change.
- A local Chromium singleton PID is stale only if `/proc/PID` itself is
  absent. Existing zombie PIDs with no `/proc/PID/exe` now keep ownership
  conservatively busy; synthetic unreaped-process evidence reproduces the
  former false-stale case without launching a real browser.
- Legacy event count-change arrays now require object-shaped entries,
  rejecting null, scalar, or nested-array items before reducing the response
  to a content-free change-presence signal. Synthetic fixtures cover both
  `MessageCounts` and `ConversationCounts` without retaining their contents.
- Lease creation now establishes its identity-bound cleanup guard
  immediately after exclusive inode creation. Write/sync/finalization
  errors now run the identity-bound `Drop` cleanup instead of stranding
  a partially initialized owner record. Existing inode replacement tests
  also guard cleanup from removing any unrelated final entry.
- The stricter two-projection content check also applies to proven
  individual-message pages, not only generic mailbox rows. A separate
  forced-message-mode fake Chromium test changes the subject mid-read and
  rejects the result without exposing a message snapshot.
- Local automation lease records now require canonical single-line syntax,
  including exact spaces and terminating newline. Noncanonical whitespace
  remains a corrupt owner entry rather than a silently reclaimed lease.
- Mailbox snapshots now revalidate the authenticated shell after bounded
  content projection. Synthetic mid-read login expiry with unchanged list IDs
  previously returned rows; it now fails as `MailShellNotReady` before release.
- Audited visible-row content projection now requires aria-labelled headings
  to be descendants of their respective row. Broken/global ARIA labels cannot
  project unrelated page titles; the static CDP script authority test enforces
  the containment guard without reading live message content.
- Audited mailbox-list and row-content selectors now refuse multiple
  matching loaded/list-loading roots instead of silently projecting only the
  first DOM match. Static CDP authority tests enforce root uniqueness.
- Mailbox pagination evidence now rejects duplicate Next buttons or
  concurrently active current-page markers, rather than taking the first
  global DOM match. Static CDP authority tests prove selector uniqueness.
- Legacy mailbox event refresh handling now treats previously unknown
  non-contact refresh bits as cursor invalidation. Contacts-only bit `2`
  remains ignorable; synthetic unknown-bit fixtures refuse stale cursors.
- Visible-page reads also re-observe list state after the final Mail-shell
  check. Synthetic pagination/row drift during shell revalidation now fails
  before a page snapshot is released, although this is not an immutable read.
- Both audited read-only DOM expressions now refuse password or one-time
  code inputs before extracting list metadata or row content. This prevents
  an overlay-based login expiry from passing via residual Mail landmarks;
  the static expression test also proves no input values are queried.
- A synthetic next-page navigation now proves that login expiry immediately
  after clicking Next cannot return message rows. Read-only pagination obeys
  the same post-projection authenticated-shell guard as initial page reads.
- Every audited read-only navigation/sort click now refuses controls with
  no rendered layout rectangle or within an `aria-hidden` subtree. The
  script-authority regression checks both guards precede each click.
- Unknown core-event refresh bits are now also exercised on an in-flight
  continuation: they reject the page without mutating the acknowledged
  event cursor or prematurely settling the change sequence.
- Content-free list pagination also requires Next controls to be rendered,
  AX-visible HTML buttons before reporting a next-page state. The existing
  audited navigation click uses the same control semantics.
- Observed Next controls and audited navigation/sort clicks also refuse
  CSS `visibility:hidden` elements that still have a layout rectangle;
  the static script tests guard this condition across all four expressions.
- Message-mode location evidence now accepts only canonical bounded,
  positive safe-integer search flags. Partial JavaScript `parseInt` matches
  (like `7junk`) and oversized/hex/signed fragments cannot authorize the
  individual-message interpretation of visible mailbox row IDs.
- Message-mode search evidence now rejects duplicate recognized fragment
  keys, whitespace-only input, control characters, and oversized search
  text instead of granting message-row semantics from ambiguous URL values.
- Visible row projection now accepts exactly one row-local ARIA heading
  and exactly one rendered sender-address title, rather than projecting the
  first of multiple matches. It also enforces the 200-row cap before content
  mapping; static CDP authority tests reject global heading lookups.
- Visible and managed browser launches now share a byte-preserving Unix
  `--user-data-dir` argument builder. Non-UTF-8 XDG data-home paths remain
  exact through environment resolution, profile setup, Chrome launch, and
  managed lease custody; process-level synthetic tests inspect raw argv.
- An explicit non-UTF-8 browser override now fails closed rather than
  silently discovering a different executable on PATH; a synthetic PATH
  candidate demonstrates the previously unintended browser fallback.
- Synthetic login also verifies raw HOME bytes survive the XDG-unset
  fallback and reach Chromium unchanged; no replacement UTF-8 profile is made.
- The content-bearing row expression now declares its row collection
  lexically rather than assigning a browser-window global. A red-first
  static authority test prevents read-only DOM inspection from mutating
  page execution state through an undeclared JavaScript variable.
- Content-free list evidence now refuses simultaneous loading and loaded
  roots during React transitions instead of silently choosing the loaded
  tree and potentially exposing an unsettled mailbox snapshot.
- Both mailbox DOM read expressions now verify exact Proton Mail origin
  inside the same JavaScript task as row/list observation. This closes a
  short redirect race between CDP origin preflight and content projection.
- Mailbox mode and both sort-evidence reads also verify exact live Mail
  origin within their individual JS tasks. Off-origin evidence cannot imply
  a mode, newest-first sort, or safely closed sort menu after redirects.
- Message-mode URL fragments now have an explicit 8192-character ceiling
  and reject malformed percent escapes before `URLSearchParams` can accept
  a partially decoded search that would otherwise prove message mode.
- Audited navigation and sort buttons also fail closed when computed
  CSS reports `pointer-events:none` or zero opacity, not just hidden
  visibility or missing rectangles; read-side Next evidence agrees.
- A sort menu observation with `ready:false` must contain no selected
  state or `valid` flags. Malformed closed-menu evidence now fails closed
  rather than authorizing an extra UI click to reopen the controls.
- Mailbox mode rejects replacement-character U+FFFD in decoded search
  text; malformed UTF-8 percent encodings cannot masquerade as a
  meaningful search through URLSearchParams' lossy decoding.
- Content-bearing row projection requires the chosen subject and sender
  elements to have rendered layout, visible computed CSS, and no ARIA-hidden
  ancestor. Hidden decoy metadata may not cross the read boundary.
- An explicit empty-list title counts as evidence only when it has a
  unique rendered node with visible computed CSS and no ARIA-hidden
  ancestor. Duplicate or hidden empty markers cannot report empty Mail.
- List-row ID extraction now requires every loaded region to be visibly
  rendered and not inside ARIA-hidden ancestry. A hidden duplicate/decoy
  row cannot enter content-free list or pagination evidence.
- Current-page selectors must also be visibly rendered and not inside
  ARIA-hidden ancestry before supplying page-number/pagination evidence;
  offscreen or hidden markers cannot authorize a Next transition.
- Newest-first sort evidence now requires every sort option to be
  visibly rendered, with nonhidden CSS and accessibility ancestry, before
  trusting `aria-pressed`. Hidden menu options cannot establish ordering.
- Sort-menu toggle now checks all four option controls are uniquely and
  visibly present immediately before closing; it will not reopen a menu
  that vanished between read evidence and the toggle click. Opening also
  rejects duplicate/partial option sets rather than counting first matches.
- Content-free message-mode and sort inspections now refuse password or
  one-time-code challenge overlays within their JavaScript reads, matching
  mailbox list/content and all audited click preconditions.
- Valid sort observations must now have precisely the six fields owned by
  the audited projection. Unknown extra state cannot be smuggled into a
  successful sort-mode proof through malformed CDP results.
- Forced-message mode now requires an exact route shape, optionally under
  one canonical bounded `/u/ACCOUNT/` prefix. Nested, padded-account, or
  doubled-slash paths cannot inherit message-mode authority accidentally.
- Mailbox-list CDP values now require exactly the eight fields emitted
  by the audited list expression, even for loading states. Extra unknown
  fields cannot silently enter settled or loading evidence.
- Message-mode CDP evidence must contain exactly the two audited booleans,
  with no unexpected fields. A red-first test rejects altered projections
  before they can grant message-row interpretation.
- Each content-bearing mailbox-row projection now requires precisely
  its four audited fields (`id`, `subject`, `addresses`, `unread`). Unknown
  nested response fields cannot silently pass through visible-page reads.
- Navigation receipts are also schema-bound: the sort toggle returns
  exactly two audited booleans, and Next-page activation returns exactly
  three. Unexpected response fields cannot authorize a successful UI click.
- Audited sort toggle clicks reject disabled controls. All read-only
  sort/pagination clicks now recheck for dialog or alertdialog overlays
  within their final JavaScript task, closing a post-AX-check race.
- Attachment-metadata MIME projections now reject header controls and
  invalid media-type essence (including missing type/subtype), before
  returning a provider-declared MIME field to downstream read workflows.
- A pre-opened sort menu is now recognized only if all four unique option
  controls have visible layout and are not in ARIA-hidden ancestry. Hidden
  duplicate/stale menus cannot masquerade as an interactive sort state.
- Message-detail MIME and inline attachment-descriptor MIME now reuse
  the same safe media-type validator as attachment metadata. Control or
  header-injection strings cannot cross either message read boundary.
- Message-detail sender and recipient fields now reject raw control
  characters and surrounding address whitespace before exposure to
  read-only workflows or possible later reply-composer handoff.
- Real JavaScript parsing exposed a malformed comma between two `const`
  declarations in the audited visible-row projection. Chromium would reject
  the task; it now uses separate statements, with a red-first regression.
- A local real-JavaScript smoke compiled all eight audited read/sort
  expressions in Node and confirmed that each denies foreign origins. A
  synthetic DOM then proved visible subject/sender projection and rejection
  of hidden headings; this complements (not replaces) Chromium acceptance.
- Message-mode proof now requires exact valid Mail route structure
  even when a recognized search fragment is present. Node synthetic
  cases cover padded and nested account paths plus valid routes.
- The eight audited read-and-sort JavaScript scripts are compiled and
  denied on foreign origins by a Node-backed regression when available;
  minimal Rust-only environments may skip the optional Node check.
- The exact message-list Network diagnostic rejects percent-encoded
  query keys, including raw aliases of `Sort`, `Page`, and `BeginID`.
  Provider and adapter cannot disagree about critical selector names.
- Pagination and sort clicks now refuse ARIA-disabled controls.
- Node DOM test confirms visible row projection and hidden-heading refusal.
- Three Network body-envelope decoders now reject unrecognized fields.
- Synthetic Chromium fixtures now install through synchronized staged files
  and atomic replacement, never opening the executable inode for writing.
  A deterministic test previously reproduced Linux `ETXTBSY` under a held
  writer. Managed launch retries only that exact OS errno for at most twelve
  10 ms intervals, while retaining its exclusive profile lease; persistent
  text-busy and other launch errors remain fail-closed. Synthetic tests
  prove temporary recovery and failure/lease cleanup after exhaustion.
- A Node-executed synthetic list DOM now checks settled row IDs, current
  page, loaded/loading overlap, hidden rows, and ARIA-disabled pagination.
  This exposed a last-page bug: a genuinely disabled Next button with CSS
  `pointer-events:none` was rejected as incompatible. The content-free read
  now recognizes it as disabled, while enabled noninteractive controls
  remain incompatible and no click authorization is broadened.
- A real-JavaScript synthetic sort-menu test verifies exactly one click
  per open/close transition and refuses clicks through disabled controls,
  hidden menu options, or modal overlays.
- Loading-list evidence now validates the types and bounds of all eight
  audited projection fields before discarding partial rows and pagination.
  A valid transient loading state still exposes no row IDs or page grants.
- Active search only proves message-row mode on an explicit set of known
  mailbox views. Unknown or unrelated Mail routes with valid search hashes
  remain unproven, including routes whose URL structure otherwise looks valid.
- Shared message/attachment MIME validation now parses bounded media-type
  parameters instead of accepting any printable suffix. It rejects missing
  names/values, duplicate case-insensitive keys, bad quoting, and unfinished
  parameters while supporting token parameters and escaped quoted values.
- A Node-executed Next-page click test now verifies an enabled control
  receives exactly one click. Disabled, hidden, duplicated, modal-blocked,
  and off-origin controls never trigger an interaction.
- All eight decoded CDP `Network.getResponseBody` projections now demand
  exactly the owned `body` and `base64Encoded` fields, including conversation
  list/detail, catalog, and bootstrap/poll event response bodies. Extra CDP
  response fields fail closed rather than silently expanding metadata scope.
- Opacity-zero decoy controls/content are now rejected by subject and
  sender row projections, loaded list rows, explicit-empty markers, current
  page markers, and sort selections. Node-executed synthetic DOM fixtures
  exercise opacity-based hiding independently from layout and ARIA hiding.
- Content-bearing subject/sender reads now prove computed visibility and
  positive opacity throughout the bounded ancestor chain. A transparent
  parent cannot expose decoy message content even if the child renders a
  layout box. Synthetic Node tests reject opacity-zero ancestors and DOM
  ancestry exceeding the audited 64-element limit.
- Content-free list observations now check root, row, empty marker,
  current-page marker, and Next-control ancestor opacity and visibility
  through a bounded chain. Transparent parent panels and implausibly deep
  ancestry cannot establish a settled mailbox or trusted pagination.
- Sort-menu open/close, sort selection, and Next-page click now verify
  bounded visibility and positive opacity along each control's ancestor
  chain before trusting ordering or clicking. Node-executed regressions
  prove transparent parent containers cannot authorize UI interactions.
- Audited DOM reads now reject oversized text and IDs inside Chromium
  before serializing results to the CDP pipe. Subject and sender text use
  the Rust byte ceilings (16 KiB), and list/content row IDs use 512 UTF-8
  bytes; page selectors must match the bounded canonical page ID pattern.
- The remaining content-free mode, list, sort, and content-bearing row
  expressions now recheck for a newly mounted dialog/alertdialog inside the
  same JavaScript task. Node-based tests prove no content or mode signal
  crosses a modal even after an earlier Mail-shell AX validation.
- Audited content reads and read-only controls now also reject `inert`
  ancestors (in addition to aria-hidden, geometry, and computed styling).
  Synthetic Node DOM cases prove inert mailbox rows, Next, and sort controls
  cannot be read or clicked during a disabled background transition.
- A search hash grants message-row mode only when **every** recognized
  text and numeric selector has a valid value. Valid `keyword` evidence
  cannot mask malformed `begin`, `end`, `from`, or `to` values; Node tests
  cover mixed valid/invalid and multiple legitimate filters.
- Decoded search selectors reject hidden formatting characters,
  bidirectional overrides, and unsafe control code points as mode proofs.
  Synthetic regressions still accept legitimate Unicode search terms.
- Next-page observation now honors an `aria-disabled` ancestor,
  including when CSS also makes the control noninteractive. The related
  click guards refuse inherited disabled state for Next and sort toggles.
- Captured conversation-list request parsing now refuses percent-encoded
  query keys. Encoded `Page`, `Sort`, or anchor aliases cannot be interpreted
  differently by the provider and the local pagination observer.
- Conversation-list sorting now rejects duplicate, invalid, or
  differently cased `Sort`/`Desc` parameters before treating the captured
  request as page evidence. Canonical allowed ordering remains supported.
- Message-list capture now rejects differently cased aliases of all
  audited pagination/sort selector names. This matches the conversation-list
  boundary and prevents ambiguous provider versus adapter interpretation.
- The browser-side DOM projection now refuses unsafe provider row ID
  characters, encoded path separators, invisible formatting, and dot-only
  aliases before they can traverse CDP. Synthetic Node tests cover both
  rejected aliases and valid Unicode IDs; Rust repeats the ID checks.
- Native HTML fieldset disabling is now observed via `:disabled`,
  not only a button's own `disabled` or ARIA state. The Next-page evidence,
  navigation click, and sort-menu click all refuse inherited disabled
  fieldsets, with red-first synthetic live-JavaScript regressions.
- Search-mode proofs now reject encoded query-key aliases and
  noncanonical case variants of recognized filter keys. URL-encoded query
  values remain allowed, including encoded spaces and separators.
- Sort-menu option reads and cleanup clicks also reject ARIA-disabled
  parent subtrees. This prevents a disabled menu from establishing visible
  sort evidence or authorizing its otherwise enabled toggle.
- Atomic DOM read and click probes now block native open `dialog`
  elements and `aria-modal` overlays as well as explicit dialog roles.
  Synthetic Node regressions verify new native dialogs interrupt visible
  message content, list state, sort state, and mode observations.
- Event-watermark bootstrap and polling now reject malformed
  `MessageCounts`/`ConversationCounts` query flags, especially values that
  silently disable a stream. Positive canonical count flags remain accepted;
  network capture rejects malformed event selectors before tracking them.
- The Rust-visible row projection now independently validates opaque IDs
  with the shared safe-ID predicate before retaining CDP content. Synthetic
  unit tests proved the previous second-layer validator accepted slash,
  encoded and invisible ID aliases; the browser-list validator had masked
  this defect in end-to-end reads. The two guards now agree.
- Content-bearing row projection now refuses embedded C0/C1 controls
  and Unicode line-separator characters in visible subject/address text.
  Browser-side preflight prevents those values crossing CDP; a separate
  Rust validator rejects malformed projections even if browser guards fail.
  Red-first synthetic fixtures cover both layers and ordinary Unicode text.
- Explicit-empty message reconciliation now requires every observed
  provider-list batch to report zero `Total`, not just the first batch.
  A synthetic regression proved that an initial zero followed by a positive
  total previously escaped as an empty mailbox despite contradictory data.
- Authenticated mailbox/label catalog continuation state now requires
  a whole-page-aligned offset. Malformed mid-page offsets cannot reissue
  overlapping catalog items even if a cursor were decoded from an unexpected
  state. Synthetic tests retain valid aligned offsets and reject gaps.
- Message metadata reconciliation now accepts at most the two supported
  browser-owned list batches (initial page plus one anchored continuation).
  A synthetic zero-row three-batch chain previously escaped as a valid
  empty page, despite lacking audited pagination/snapshot evidence.
- During an active list-loading state, Chromium now emits only a fixed
  content-free loading object instead of collecting transient row identifiers
  or pagination attributes. A Node-executed DOM regression throws on any
  attempted loading-row read and confirms no IDs cross CDP.
- Rust now accepts only the exact fixed loading projection emitted by
  Chromium: empty row IDs, zero skeleton count, no empty marker, and no
  pagination state. A mutated loading response with transitional fields
  fails closed instead of quietly discarding the unauthorized values.
- Browser-owned Time sorting now rejects an equal-Time, equal-Order
  pair of distinct message IDs. Such a pair lacks a proven stable tie-break
  and cannot establish deterministic batch or page ordering until an
  independently validated provider-ID normalization is available.
- A synthetic sort comparator matrix now covers ten descending and
  ascending cases, including precedence of unequal `Time` over `Order`,
  strict `Order` ties, reversed order, and exact secondary collisions.
- Content-bearing browser projections now preflight all visible row IDs
  for uniqueness, byte bounds, and safe opaque naming before accessing any
  subject or sender attribute. The whole JavaScript result is also null if
  even one row's metadata is malformed, preventing valid sibling content
  from entering CDP on an ultimately rejected read. Content-free list
  projections reject duplicate IDs in Chromium before serialization too.
  Node-backed red-first regressions check non-exposure in both cases.
- Atomic content-bearing row projection now refuses a concurrently
  mounted `message-list-loading` subtree before touching message text,
  even if an old loaded list remains in the DOM. A red-first Node fixture
  verifies it does not read headings under the loading overlay, and the
  synthetic browser harness separates row and list expression branches so
  the final-shell list-drift regression keeps testing the correct boundary.
- Exact-full final message-list batches now return without awaiting an
  unnecessary empty anchored continuation when the provider Total already
  proves the observed page boundary. A simultaneously observed extra request
  still fails closed as ambiguous. Synthetic full-CDP integration regressions
  cover both cases, and fixture routing distinguishes list from content tasks.
- Captured anchored message-list continuations now reject provider IDs
  already present in the initial batch, even though prefetched continuation
  rows cannot satisfy visible current-page coverage. A red-first complete
  synthetic CDP transcript exposed an accepted duplicate that first-batch
  reconciliation never examined.
- Visible Next-page evidence now must agree with an exact provider
  message-list Total: an enabled Next button when `Total` has already
  exhausted the current request page fails closed rather than publishing a
  misleading cursor. Synthetic tests cover the contradictory final-page
  state and retain an enabled Next when provider Total proves more rows.
- Synthetic message-mode page fixtures now route audited row projection
  commands before content-free list probes: the row expression itself checks
  the loading selector, so the old fixture order falsely classified content
  tasks as list reads. Nine synthetic message-page regressions pass again.
- Synthetic message pagination now dispatches row projection before
  generic content-free list probes. The content script itself checks for
  loading overlap; fixture order previously masked all eight pagination
  outcomes. The restored suite verifies Next, drift and login-expiry cases.
- Message-mode search proof now refuses Unicode Hangul fillers (U+115F,
  U+1160, U+3164, U+FFA0) and the blank Braille cell (U+2800), whose decoded
  values are nonempty but visually blank. A red-first Node-executed location
  matrix proves none can imply a verified individual-message search surface.

- Both browser-owned visible-row ID preflights and the shared Rust opaque
  read-ID guard now reject additional invisible Unicode fillers and variation
  selectors. Red-first Rust and Node fixtures prove a blank-looking message ID
  previously passed even though search-mode validation correctly rejected its
  Unicode class. Conversation, catalog, event-watermark and detail regressions
  exercise the shared validator throughout read-only metadata boundaries.
- Web event cursor encoding now rejects invalid adapter generation tags
  before producing ciphertext. The public scope permits arbitrary opaque
  generation strings, but event tokens require canonical 32-character
  lowercase hex; a red-first codec regression proves malformed values no
  longer create tokens the decoder can never resume.
- All eight passive CDP Network request trackers now apply the same
  512-byte printable-ASCII request-ID rule as the audited outbound body
  command. Red-first regression tests refuse malformed IDs before capture
  while ordinary dotted browser request IDs remain valid.
- Role-filtered Mail shell queries now reject explicitly contradictory
  `AXNode.role` values (including malformed role objects) before classifying
  navigation, search, or dialog visibility. They retain compatibility with
  Chromium results omitting the optional role field. A red-first fake-CDP
  suite proves rejection without projecting accessible names or mailbox data.
- Search-mode proof now rejects Mongolian and presentation variation
  selectors, including supplementary selector range U+E0100-U+E01EF.
  Node-executed red-first URL fragment fixtures show these visually blank
  values previously established search mode despite no visible query text.
- Still required: prove an immutable snapshot boundary, extend bounded
  equal-Time ID normalization into any future cursor chain, then implement a
  durable cursor/snapshot chain,
  prove independently safe `list_threads` semantics for grouping-capable
  locations, add composer/mutation workflows and remaining control-level drift
  fixtures, and capture the opted-in Free-account acceptance transcript.

- Message-list observation refuses a redirected-in exact endpoint even when
  the earlier URL was never tracked: CDP reuses request IDs across redirect
  hops, so a redirected GET cannot prove a fresh provider list. A synthetic
  regression covers that previously accepted lifecycle.

- Conversation-list and exact conversation-detail Network observers now also
  reject redirected-in GETs whose original URL was ignored by the tracker.
  Synthetic lifecycle tests reproduce both previously accepted hops.

- Exact message-detail, attachment-metadata, and mailbox/label catalog GET
  observations likewise refuse CDP requests redirected from an ignored URL.
  Synthetic retry/capture suites now cover the redirected-in ambiguity.

- Both event-watermark observations (latest bootstrap and core-v5 polling) now
  refuse redirected-in GETs, preventing an ignored source endpoint from being
  treated as newly proven mailbox cursor evidence. A synthetic regression
  exercises both state machines.

- A synthetic DevTools-pipe browser regression now injects a redirected-in
  message-list GET on reload and proves typed refusal, Network cleanup before
  any body fetch, and a still-usable CDP session.

- Browser-side search-mode and visible-list ID guards now reject the Mongolian
  Free Variation Selector Four (U+180F), previously omitted from the adjacent
  U+180B–U+180E invisible-selector range. The shared Rust route-ID guard agrees;
  red-first Node-executed and Rust synthetic regressions show this invisible
  character can no longer prove search mode or pass as an opaque row ID.

- Mailbox list, row, mode, pagination, and sort evidence now compare protective
  `role` and `aria-*` selector values without ASCII case sensitivity. Chromium
  treats these attribute values as case-sensitive CSS selectors by default;
  uppercase modal/hidden/disabled values could otherwise bypass the adapter's
  DOM safety checks. Native button-disabled checks remain authoritative and
  explicit `aria-disabled` reads now treat uppercase `TRUE` as disabling.
  Synthetic Node regressions first reproduced modal and button bypasses, then
  verified case-variant dialog, hidden-subtree, pagination, and sort guards.

- Protective dialog and alertdialog checks now match individual whitespace-
  delimited ARIA role tokens rather than requiring exact `role` equality.
  A fallback role list can expose a dialog in the accessibility tree while an
  exact CSS selector misses it; synthetic modal evidence now requires the
  case-insensitive token selector to block all audited DOM reads and clicks.

- Opaque read IDs and audited rendered-list/row JS expressions now reject
  U+FFFD, the Unicode replacement character, before a lossy provider ID
  can be used for an exact route or exposed in CDP metadata. Synthetic
  red-first tests preserve ordinary Unicode identity support.

- Flattened CDP command replies now require the exact attached session ID;
  browser-scoped replies must have no session ID. This prevents a reply from
  another scope, or an omitted scope, from acknowledging a Mail read. Synthetic
  Chromium fixtures now emit session IDs on attached commands, and regressions
  prove the driver drains a spoofed reply, fails closed, cleans up Network,
  and remains usable for later safe inspection.

- CDP command-reply envelopes now refuse unrecognized top-level fields even
  when their command ID and session match. A red-first synthetic browser reply
  carrying an extra private field can no longer authorize Network enable;
  the real reply is drained before cleanup and later safe inspection remains
  usable.

- Unsolicited CDP notifications now reject unexpected top-level envelope fields
  before any passive Network observer sees them. Red-first synthetic frames
  proved that unknown extension fields (including explicit nulls) previously
  passed as harmless events and let a message-list read succeed; strict
  validation now fails closed, drains the genuine command reply, disables
  Network, and keeps the managed session usable.

- Browser `Runtime.evaluate` replies now check any explicit CDP
  `RemoteObject.type` against the returned by-value JSON before it can prove
  a Mail origin or UI state. A red-first synthetic origin fixture showed
  that a `string`-typed response carrying an object previously authorized
  a Mail page. Contradictory, unknown and malformed types fail closed;
  existing fixtures omitting the optional type stay compatible.

- CDP command replies now validate the method-specific result body as well
  as the outer envelope. `Target.attachToTarget` returns exactly one safe
  session handle; `Network.enable`/`disable`, `Page.reload`, browser close,
  and target detach require empty result objects. Red-first synthetic tests
  proved extra nested fields previously authorized a trusted page and a
  passive Network capture; the latter still disables Network on rejection.

- Runtime by-value evidence now also rejects contradictory or unsupported CDP
  `RemoteObject.subtype` metadata: only `array` and `null` subtypes matching
  their actual JSON types can accompany an object result. A red-first fake
  browser proved that an origin object explicitly tagged as array previously
  passed trusted-origin verification; the new guard fails closed.

- Shared Rust provider-identity validation and browser-owned visible-list,
  visible-row, and search-mode JavaScript now reject two additional classes
  of default-ignorable Unicode: Khmer inherent vowel signs U+17B4–U+17B5
  and shorthand format controls U+1BCA0–U+1BCA3. Red-first tests proved the
  old guards accepted these hidden marks as route-bound opaque IDs and as
  browser search evidence. All boundaries still accept ordinary Unicode.

- Visible Mail DOM row-ID guards now reject unpaired UTF-16 surrogate code
  units *before* inspecting row subjects, senders, or serializing CDP JSON.
  `TextEncoder` silently replaces malformed surrogate input, so relying only
  on post-CDP Rust UTF-8 validation could cross a content-bearing boundary.
  Red-first synthetic Node tests verify both list and row projection reject
  malformed surrogates without rejecting valid supplementary-plane characters.

- Active-search message-mode proof now requires every URL hash key to be an
  exactly recognized search selector. Previously an unknown `view=threads`
  or future provider option could accompany a valid `keyword` and still
  authorize individual-message rows even though the option's effect was
  unknown. Red-first browser JS tests cover unknown, empty, and trailing
  hash keys while retaining the audited search selectors and forced routes.

- Search-mode evidence now refuses inverted `begin`/`end` date ranges,
  even when accompanied by a valid keyword or placed on a forced message
  route. Red-first browser JavaScript cases show that the contradictory
  range previously proved message mode; ordered or equal boundaries still
  work, while impossible intervals fail closed.

- Search-mode evidence now recognizes only `wildcard=1` as a supported
  modifier; noncanonical numeric values such as `wildcard=2` or `99` fail
  closed, even alongside a valid keyword/date or on forced message routes.
  Red-first live-JavaScript cases proved the previous false authorization;
  ordinary text/date searches and the supported modifier still work.

- A standalone `wildcard` search-hash modifier no longer proves active
  individual-message search mode. It must accompany a valid textual or
  `begin`/`end` date filter; malformed modifiers still invalidate all search
  evidence. Red-first Node cases proved the prior lone `wildcard=1` false
  authorization and preserve genuine text/date-only searches.

- Browser message-mode proof now refuses an unaudited URL query string and
  trailing-slash route variants. Red-first Node fixtures previously showed
  that those locations could still authorize individual-message reads from
  an otherwise forced or active-search route; canonical paths and hash-only
  search filters remain supported.

- Forced message routes no longer bypass malformed hash-filter validation.
  Unrecognized keys, duplicate selectors, hidden characters and invalid
  filters now refuse mode evidence even on Sent/Drafts; canonical forced
  routes without hashes and supported filters still prove message mode.
  Red-first live-JavaScript fixtures exposed the former bypass.

- Rust route-ID validation and browser-owned list, row, and search guards now
  refuse all Unicode 16 Cf formatting controls, including Arabic and Syriac
  markers, interlinear annotations, Kaithi and Egyptian hieroglyph controls,
  and musical format controls. Red-first Node and metadata fixtures prove
  that the previously omitted marks could cross exact-ID/search boundaries;
  legitimate printable Unicode remains accepted.

- Dedicated browser-profile setup now refuses a selected XDG/HOME data-home
  directory with group or world write permissions before resolving the fixed
  project/profile children. Another account could otherwise rename a private
  project directory between validation and Chromium launch. Red-first fake
  browser login tests for 0770 and 0777 verify no launch and no chmod of the
  caller-selected data home; private data homes remain supported.

- Browser-profile custody now also checks trusted owner and shared-write/sticky
  permissions on every ancestor of the selected and resolved XDG/HOME data
  home. A private 0700 leaf under a non-sticky 0770/0777 parent was previously
  accepted and would permit another local account to replace the path before
  Chromium launch. Red-first process tests prove refusal through both a direct
  XDG home and a symlink alias, without changing caller-owned directories;
  ordinary private parents and trusted sticky ancestors remain usable.

- Content-bearing Mail row reads now reject isolated UTF-16 surrogate code
  units in displayed subject and address attributes before CDP serialization.
  `TextEncoder` silently replaces malformed surrogates with U+FFFD, so a
  browser-owned preflight is needed even with Rust-side UTF-8 validation.
  Red-first Node DOM regressions cover both fields and retain valid emoji.

- Catalog response projection now refuses terminal control bytes (except the
  previously supported embedded LF) and Unicode bidirectional overrides in
  untrusted folder/label names. Red-first synthetic label/folder cases proved
  escapes could reach the retained paging snapshot; exact display names still
  preserve ordinary Unicode, emoji selectors, embedded LF, and NBSP without
  rewriting provider text. This does not yet expose public catalog tools.

- Dedicated browser profile preparation now preflights the nearest existing
  XDG/HOME data-home ancestor before recursively creating a missing selected
  home. Previously an unsafe non-sticky shared-writable parent was rejected
  only after mkdir left private-looking directories in that untrusted tree.
  Red-first process tests prove no directory or browser launch side effect for
  rejected paths and retain creation under trusted sticky parents.

- Follow-up catalog display regression: Unicode paragraph/line separators,
  zero-width or soft-hyphen marks, and remaining invisible format controls
  also fail closed instead of being exported as folder/label display names.
  This aligns the catalog guard with existing visible-message text rules while
  retaining explicitly supported LF, NBSP, emoji ZWJ and variation selectors.

- Visible mailbox rows, message-detail subject/recipient names, and catalog
  folder/label names now reuse one private Unicode display-safety predicate.
  Previously three near-identical copies could drift independently; catalog
  retains its single historical LF exception. Direct synthetic Unicode tests
  and existing per-adapter fixtures lock hidden-control refusal and emoji
  joiner/selector compatibility without expanding the public adapter API.

- Catalog cursor encoding likewise uses borrowed account/snapshot references
  and serializes directly into a zeroizing plaintext buffer before AES-GCM.
  This avoids transient ordinary-heap JSON copies of private scope values,
  including the serialization-error path; cursor decoding is unchanged.

- Authenticated catalog cursor decode now uses the same drop-time sensitive
  JSON scrubber as event cursors, so temporary account/snapshot copies are
  zeroized after validation or a scope error without changing cursor framing.

- Both bounded CDP response frame readers now allocate into
  `Zeroizing<Vec<u8>>` immediately. Raw browser/provider bytes are scrubbed
  when the frame is parsed or a malformed, oversized, or partial frame fails;
  the waiting reader also scrubs when it times out after receiving bytes.
  Red-first typed ownership and synthetic pipe tests cover both entry points,
  preserving NUL-delimited framing, timeouts, and bounded message handling.
  This covers retained frame allocations, not socket buffers, parsed CDP
  JSON, or provider-generated copies outside this adapter.

- Outbound CDP command construction now keeps the cloned request parameters
  and target/session identifiers in a scrub-on-drop JSON graph. The encoded
  NUL-delimited request frame also remains in a `Zeroizing<Vec<u8>>` through
  the socket write and flush, including error paths. A red-first synthetic
  encoder test asserts exact frame framing and typed ownership, preserving
  the existing audited command allowlist. OS socket buffers and original
  caller-owned parameter values are outside this local custody guarantee.

- Visible login now refuses existing or unprovable Chromium profile owners
  before spawn and during ETXTBSY retries. Reusing a live browser can bypass
  the dedicated profile privacy launch flags; synthetic singleton tests keep
  the visible and managed ownership boundaries aligned.

- Read-only Mail list evidence, next-page activation and sort-menu open/close
  now require an explicit non-submit button (`type=button`) with no form
  owner. A changed provider control with the same `data-testid` could
  otherwise submit a form during a navigation click despite visibility
  checks. Red-first Rust and synthetic Node regressions prove rejection of
  submit and form-associated buttons; ordinary pagination and sort toggles
  remain supported. This does not authorize any mailbox mutation.

- Managed browser automation now retains an exclusive advisory lock on the
  private project-directory inode for the whole automation lease lifetime.
  Cooperative stale-lease recovery, creation and identity-checked `Drop`
  unlink therefore cannot interleave at a replaceable lease pathname.
  Visible login also refuses the directory lock during the pre-file
  acquisition window. Red-first synthetic contention and CLI tests prove
  all three cases, retaining stale recovery after process exit; cooperation
  is required because unrelated same-UID processes can still alter paths.

- Provider target discovery and Runtime.evaluate now require exact audited
  CDP result keys, not only valid values within an extended object. Red-first
  synthetic Chromium replies previously selected a Mail page and proved its
  origin despite unexpected sibling fields; both now fail closed. A distinct
  legacy `wasThrown: false` runtime reply remains accepted. This does not
  change target metadata handling or enable a live Mail account.

- Browser CDP shell inspection now also requires exact result objects for
  `DOM.getDocument` and `Accessibility.queryAXTree` before using the root node
  or role-filtered landmarks. Red-first fake Chromium replies with extra
  sibling properties previously proved a ready shell; both now fail closed
  before reading message metadata. Exact canonical results remain supported.

- Provider target discovery now refuses Mail/Account tabs that declare
  another debugger already attached (`TargetInfo.attached: true`), malformed
  attachment-state types, or malformed browser context IDs. Red-first synthetic
  browser catalogs previously selected attached targets. Chromium can include
  `browserContextId` even for ordinary profile tabs; validating its shape alone
  cannot establish default-profile ownership.

- For a selected Mail/Account target with `browserContextId`, the adapter now
  queries the read-only `Target.getBrowserContexts` command and requires an
  explicit matching `defaultBrowserContextId`. Unrelated contexts, malformed
  or duplicate context IDs, overlapping context lists, and extra CDP result
  keys fail closed. Context evidence stays attached to the selected target
  entry even when an unrelated target reuses its ID.

- Red-first synthetic CDP replies established both the context-selection and
  duplicate-ID gaps. When older Chromium omits the optional target context ID,
  origin verification remains necessary but default-context proof from this
  method is unavailable. No account or live browser is used.

- Target discovery now also rejects repeated or invalid CDP `targetId` fields
  across the entire bounded target catalog, not only multiple provider pages.
  An unrelated worker or non-Proton tab cannot borrow the selected Mail page's
  identity to launder an ambiguous attachment. Red-first synthetic browser
  catalog cases show the prior worker/page duplicate-ID bypass; the same valid
  catalog is accepted unchanged when all listed IDs are unique.

- The shared passive CDP source gate now rejects unreviewed response field
  names containing cache, prefetch, service-worker, source, or security-state
  markers even when the marker is not a leading prefix. Red-first synthetic
  `isCached` and `responseSource` extensions previously passed as fresh HTTP;
  ordinary documented source flags still pass when explicitly false. The
  check allocates no response-field copies and applies to all read collectors.

- Nested CDP AXValue scalars now honor their declared primitive kinds before
  optional role sources can establish a visible Mail shell. Red-first fake
  Chromium fixtures previously accepted numeric `tristate`/`idref`, boolean
  `token`, textual `booleanOrUndefined`, and concrete `valueUndefined` claims.
  Realistic text tokens, boolean-or-undefined, and missing undefined values
  remain compatible. Source metadata is checked but never projected.

- Mail-shell AX landmark proof now refuses contradictory explicit Page.FrameIds
  among visible navigation/search roles. A red-first fake-browser fixture
  previously combined navigation in one frame with search in another to prove
  readiness; both roles now require the same frame when their optional frame
  IDs are present. Same-frame landmarks and older replies without frame IDs
  remain compatible; no new browser queries or accessible names are needed.

- All passive `Network.getResponseBody` replies now require the exact CDP
  `{body:string, base64Encoded:boolean}` result shape at the shared command
  boundary. A red-first fake browser previously passed unreviewed siblings to
  the downstream list projector, which rejected them as malformed; the driver
  now rejects such metadata immediately as a protocol violation and still
  disables Network cleanly. Other body projectors retain their own checks.

- The optional CDP AXNode `chromeRole` field now requires a bounded typed
  `internalRole` AXValue with a string scalar and audited optional source and
  relationship arrays. Red-first synthetic role replies previously accepted
  null, untyped, contradictory or extended internal-role metadata as if it
  were harmless. A valid internal role may still differ from the exposed ARIA
  role, and missing Chrome role metadata remains compatible.

- Shell role queries now reject reuse of an explicit backend DOM node ID
  across distinct accessibility nodes, including ignored dialog aliases.
  Red-first fake Chromium replies previously let one DOM element claim both
  navigation and search landmarks with different AX IDs. Distinct bounded
  backend IDs remain compatible; missing optional IDs remain supported.

- Observed AX parent links now agree with any explicitly reported childIds
  list from that parent, including an empty list. Previously a navigation
  node listing no children could still be accepted as the parent of a search
  landmark in a different role reply. Red-first fake Chromium tests prove
  rejection; omitted optional lists and consistent lists still work.

- Visible conversation-list snapshots no longer accept unknown GET query
  selectors as proof of an initial page. Red-first synthetic responses with
  new filter/sort keys previously reconciled against visible rows as if the
  request were canonical. Passive diagnostics remain possible, while known
  Keyword/LabelID filters and ordinary page/sort selectors stay compatible.

- Conversation-list Keyword/LabelID filters now require canonical casing and
  unique keys. Red-first visible-page fixtures previously accepted two
  competing filter values (or case-variant aliases) as a normal initial page;
  both now fail during request parsing before any response body is read.

- Passive message-list GET capture now rejects noncanonical case variants of
  recognized `LabelID`, `Keyword`, and `Unread` filters. Red-first requests
  with an alternate case (including mixed canonical/aliased LabelID keys)
  previously passed the batch-shape classifier; these now fail before capture
  while canonical repeated LabelID values remain accepted and ordered.

- Standalone message-list `Begin` and `End` range selectors now disqualify
  a request from visible batch capture even without `BeginID`/`EndID`.
  Red-first synthetic Network GET lifecycles previously completed as a normal
  initial batch. The observer now ignores these recovery-range responses,
  preserving normal initial and continuation pagination without body reads.

- Message-list captures now reject repeated scalar `Keyword` and `Unread`
  selectors before any request can become a tracked provider batch.
  Red-first requests with conflicting scalar values were accepted previously;
  multi-valued canonical `LabelID` continues to support ordered repeats.

- Passive message-list initial and anchored continuation capture now refuses
  unreviewed query keys before they can count as visible batch evidence.
  Red-first synthetic HTTP-200 completion previously accepted added future
  filter/sort selectors; canonical LabelID, Keyword, and Unread filters remain
  compatible. Query content is never retained in diagnostics.

- Canonical `Unread` message-list filters now require exactly `0` or `1`.
  Red-first synthetic requests proved that empty, encoded, textual and out-of-
  range values previously qualified as list evidence; both boolean digits
  remain accepted without storing the private query string.

- Message-list request and direct-response URL proof now requires the
  browser-serialized ASCII graphical form. Red-first raw spaces, controls,
  Unicode whitespace and non-ASCII query text previously entered read
  metadata; percent-encoded search terms remain valid without decoding.

- Conversation-list passive capture and standalone response projection now
  require browser-serialized graphical ASCII URLs. Red-first synthetic raw
  whitespace, control and Unicode query text previously authorized a GET;
  canonical percent-encoded search terms still prove passive request shape.

- Single-message detail GET evidence now rejects raw non-ASCII URL path
  aliases without rejecting Unicode metadata IDs. Red-first synthetic
  matching provider IDs previously projected a message through a URL that
  Chromium should percent-encode; canonical ASCII detail URLs stay valid.

- Conversation-detail membership capture and direct response parsing now
  reject non-ASCII raw URL path components. Red-first synthetic Unicode
  conversation identity previously authorized complete members despite a
  noncanonical browser URL; exact ASCII paths remain supported.

- Attachment-metadata browser GET provenance and direct projection now reject
  raw Unicode URL path identities. A synthetic response using matching
  Unicode metadata IDs previously authorized a noncanonical URL; ordinary
  ASCII attachment GETs continue working with no attachment bytes retained.

- Mail core-event continuation GETs now require browser-serialized ASCII
  URLs before a requested event ID can establish polling provenance. Red-first
  raw Unicode selectors previously created apparently valid event cursors;
  canonical event URLs and bounded audited query flags remain supported.

- Passive CDP response source checks now reject non-ASCII metadata property
  names, including Unicode lookalikes of cache and security-state flags.
  Red-first direct and synthetic message-list lifecycles previously accepted
  these unaudited source extensions as fresh HTTP. Canonical ASCII CDP
  response fields remain compatible without retaining private metadata.

- Exact passive GET captures now refuse CDP Request metadata that reports a
  possible request body: `hasPostData` other than false, `postData`, or
  `postDataEntries`, even when the URL and method look read-only. The common
  body-free guard covers message/conversation lists, detail/attachment
  metadata, mailbox catalogs, and both core-event paths. Synthetic red-first
  message-list and event tests exposed the previous acceptance; negative
  `hasPostData:false` remains compatible with ordinary browser GETs.

- Passive GET provenance also now excludes speculative CDP preloads/prefetches.
  `Request.isLinkPreload`, `requestWillBeSent.type` (Prefetch/Preflight), and
  `initiator.type` (preload/preflight) are examined before tracking any exact
  app endpoint; malformed explicit markers fail closed. Red-first message-list
  and event fixtures proved a speculative GET previously looked like direct
  mailbox evidence. All eight passive captures share the predicate while
  ordinary Fetch/XHR/script and omitted legacy hints remain compatible.
